   "text": "Read the file `/var/folders/3g/vf36b0b14wzd0fgsgtdp06h00000gp/T/writing-probe/g-be0d46ca/06c26236.md` and follow it (skip this step when the file is empty). Read no other file. Write the landing page copy for `portlog`, following the tool page section order. Use only the facts in the fact sheet below; do not add facts. Reply with the page copy as Markdown and nothing else. Do not edit files, start subagents, or browse.\n\nFact sheet:\n\n1. portlog records which process held each local TCP port over time.\n2. It answers \"what was on port 3000 an hour ago?\" after the process has exited.\n3. Install: `brew install example/tap/portlog`.\n4. `portlog start` runs a background recorder. `portlog stop` stops it.\n5. `portlog who 3000` shows every process that held port 3000, newest first.\n6. `portlog who 3000 --since 2h` limits it to the last two hours.\n7. `portlog free 3000` stops the process that holds port 3000 now, after confirmation. `-y` skips the confirmation.\n8. History is kept for 7 days, in `~/.portlog/`.\n9. Runs on macOS and Linux. Written in Go. MIT license.\n10. It never records command-line arguments, because they can hold secrets.\n11. The recorder polls `lsof` every 2 seconds on macOS and reads `/proc/net/tcp` on Linux.\n12. The store is SQLite in WAL mode, with a busy timeout of 500 ms.\n13. Releases use release-please, and `fix:` makes a patch.\n14. The `who` query uses the index `idx_port_time`.",
