   "text": "# portlog\n\n## Find out what held a port—even after the process exits.\n\n“What was on port 3000 an hour ago?”\n\nportlog records which process held each local TCP port over time, so you can look it up later.\n\n## Install\n\n```sh\nbrew install example/tap/portlog\n```\n\nRuns on macOS and Linux.\n\n## Usage\n\n### Start recording\n\n```sh\nportlog start\n```\n\nRuns the recorder in the background. Stop it with:\n\n```sh\nportlog stop\n```\n\n### Look up a port’s history\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000\n```\n\nSee every recorded process that held port 3000, newest first—even processes that have exited.\n\nLimit the results to the last two hours:\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000 --since 2h\n```\n\n### Free a port\n\n```sh\nportlog free 3000\n```\n\nStops the process holding port 3000 now, after confirmation. Add `-y` to skip confirmation.\n\n## History and privacy\n\nHistory is kept for 7 days in `~/.portlog/`.\n\nportlog never records command-line arguments, because they can hold secrets.\n\n## Built with Go. MIT licensed.",
